DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/el-tiempo-no-para-memoria-y-h
 istoria-para-pensar-proyectos-de-futuro-en-el-cono-sur\n\nCo-sponsored by 
 the Consul General of Uruguay in San Francisco\n\nIn this lecture\, author
 s Aldo Marchesi y Vania Markarian will present their book El tiempo no par
 a. The book aims to trace the history of the idea of human rights and to u
 nderstand the impact of that concept on the development of democracies at 
 the close of the 20th century and in progressive governments of the 21st c
 entury.  It further puts forth questions on the limits of the language of 
 human rights when considering the contemporary crisis. \n\nAldo Marchesi h
 olds a PhD in History from New York University and is a Professor in the D
 epartment of Theory and Historiography at the Institute for History at The
  University of the Republic\, as well as a member of the Center for Urugua
 yan Interdisciplinary Studies. Together with colleagues from other discipl
 ines\, he recently founded the Institute for Research on Social Justice an
 d Inequality. He is a member of the National System of Researchers in Urug
 uay. He has served as a visiting professor at universities and research ce
 nters in the United States\, Chile\, Argentina\, Colombia\, and China. His
  work has focused on the recent history of Uruguay and the Southern Cone. 
 He has published numerous articles and books. His first book\, reissued in
  2023\, was El Uruguay Inventado: La política audiovisual durante la dict
 adura. His most recent book\, a regional history of the armed Left in the 
 Southern Cone\, was published in Spanish and English under the title Hacer
  la revolución (Maki
